1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,471
JAMIE: Trump's tariff wave just hit dozens of countries, and the dominoes are already falling—we're tracking what cracks first.

2
00:00:06,471 --> 00:00:39,853
ALEX: Markets are sending mixed signals this morning. The S&P 500 is up point one zero percent, the Dow gained point two five percent, but the Nasdaq is actually down point three three percent—and that's the real story. The V I X popped one point four four points to eighteen point nine seven, which means traders are pricing in some actual unease. Meanwhile, the Russell 2000 is up point three nine percent, so small-caps are outrunning the big tech names. That gap exists for one reason: tariff anxiety is finally hitting the sector that thought it was immune.

3
00:00:39,853 --> 00:00:56,203
JAMIE: So Nasdaq's down while the small-cap index gains—that's the tariff trade working in reverse. The companies that were supposed to benefit from reshoring and domestic supply chains are actually moving, and the mega-cap tech firms that everyone's been piling into are getting nervous. What's moving the most?

4
00:00:56,203 --> 00:01:23,113
ALEX: On the upside, Accenture is up three point four six percent, Adobe's up three point one two, Salesforce up two point nine—enterprise software is rallying because those companies help businesses manage supply chain chaos. On the downside, Broadcom's down two point three seven percent, A M D's down one point nine three. Both are semiconductor firms that depend on Asia-Pacific manufacturing and global logistics. Broadcom especially—they're a bellwether for whether supply chains are actually breaking or just getting expensive.

5
00:01:23,113 --> 00:01:43,551
MAYA: So the software companies that sell you the band-aid for your broken supply chain are going up, and the companies that actually make the chips you need are going down. It's like watching people buy fire extinguishers while the building's still on fire and the fire department hasn't even shown up yet. That's the tariff trade in real time.

6
00:01:43,551 --> 00:01:58,538
JAMIE: We've got enterprise software rallying to help fix a problem the government just created, semiconductors sliding because they're the first ones to feel the pressure. That's the tariff wave hitting the market in real time—not as a headline anymore, as actual portfolio decisions.

7
00:01:58,538 --> 00:02:06,713
MAYA: The countries hit include Vietnam, Mexico, India, Thailand—so you're talking about the entire manufacturing backbone that's been running low-cost production for two decades.

8
00:02:06,713 --> 00:02:12,845
JAMIE: So the software companies rallying because they help you manage supply chaos—they're actually racing against the clock.

9
00:02:12,845 --> 00:02:27,492
MAYA: The second-order play is actually in logistics. If tariffs hit Vietnam and Mexico hard enough, companies start reshoring or shifting to tariff-exempt countries—and suddenly you need warehouses in Ohio instead of Long Beach. That's a massive real estate and logistics infrastructure bet.

10
00:02:27,492 --> 00:02:39,414
JAMIE: So we're not watching a tariff trade anymore—we're watching a cascade of second-order bets that are being placed right now, before the first wave of price increases even lands. The market's already three moves ahead.

11
00:02:39,414 --> 00:02:54,061
MAYA: Meta's smart glasses just got banned from Comic-Con because people were secretly filming attendees without consent. Which is wild because that's not a Comic-Con problem—that's the product's entire value proposition finally meeting actual humans who don't want to be in the product.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c204le0nvn3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

12
00:02:54,061 --> 00:03:07,686
JAMIE: So Meta built glasses designed to record everything, shipped them to a convention full of people in costumes who literally came to be seen, and the convention said no. That's not a privacy glitch—that's the market rejecting the premise.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c204le0nvn3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

13
00:03:07,686 --> 00:03:19,949
ALEX: S A P beat revenue expectations, but the guidance came in cautious. When enterprise software companies get quiet about the next quarter, it's because they're watching their customer base tighten spending ahead of tariff fallout.

14
00:03:19,949 --> 00:03:31,189
JAMIE: The EU just said TikTok's kid safety features can't be opt-in anymore—which means TikTok's entire business model for Gen Z, which runs on algorithmic engagement for minors, just got a regulatory deadline.

15
00:03:31,189 --> 00:03:46,858
ALEX: Opt-in protection means the algorithm has to treat all users under eighteen as protected by default. That kills the personalization that makes TikTok addictive for that demographic. The EU basically said the product you built for kids can't actually work the way you built it.

16
00:03:46,858 --> 00:04:03,208
MAYA: L G monitors are now installing McAfee pop-ups directly onto Windows machines through the L G Monitor App Installer. Users are getting bombarded with trial ads every time they boot up, and Microsoft's Windows team had to step in and tell L G to kill the feature.

17
00:04:03,208 --> 00:04:17,174
JAMIE: So L G sold you a monitor—a physical piece of hardware you own—and then used it as a Trojan horse to install McAfee ads on your operating system. That's not a bloatware problem, that's a monitor holding your desktop hostage.

18
00:04:17,174 --> 00:04:25,349
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: American Express and Charter Communications both report on July twenty-fourth, and we've got some real tension in those numbers.

19
00:04:25,349 --> 00:04:44,765
ALEX: American Express is the canary here. They're a credit card company that lives on consumer spending and merchant volume. If tariffs are already squeezing retail margins, American Express sees it first—their transaction data is basically real-time GDP. Charter's the other way: they're a cable company, so they're watching whether households start cutting subscriptions when inflation hits.

20
00:04:44,765 --> 00:04:52,940
MAYA: And if both of them signal weakness tomorrow, that's the market realizing the tariff pain isn't three months out—it's already in the numbers.

21
00:04:52,940 --> 00:05:04,181
JAMIE: So tomorrow we're not just watching earnings—we're watching whether the Fed's inflation read stays wrong for another quarter or if the tape finally admits the tariffs are already hitting real people's wallets.

22
00:05:04,181 --> 00:05:08,950
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
